WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.090
- I got an email from a
company called Lazarus 3D

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.700
offering to show me my
own internal organs,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.500
not on a screen or visualization,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.384
but by 3D printing
a full-size, realistic, and

00:00:10.384 --> 00:00:12.270
heavy copy of my abdomen,

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.330
and cutting into it with
an actual surgical robot.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.380
Now, this isn't going to be the version

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.520
with full realistic blood.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.750
YouTube probably wouldn't like that.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.580
But, if you're scared of surgery,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.470
you might want to watch
through your fingers.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.000
And also, this isn't an advert.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.320
I paid for my own MRI scan

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.970
to get the data that they've worked with,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.110
but I'm not exactly doing

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.420
a randomized controlled trial
here either.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.890
The company wants to show off.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.270
The case studies I've seen look solid,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.390
but I am not qualified to
check medical claims.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.580
I'm just a guy who's
about to watch someone

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.270
cut into a 3D printed copy
of my own guts.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.620
- So our early studies indicate
that by performing rehearsals,

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.810
there can be clinical benefits,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.670
but that's something that's
going to need to be borne out

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.416
as we perform more and more studies.

00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:54.173
- Let's open it up.
- Let's do it.

00:00:54.173 --> 00:00:58.590
- In every other human endeavor,
an ability to rehearse and practice,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.010
and do so specifically for
that individual event,

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.390
has major payoffs.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.750
So if we take the best
violin player in the world,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.540
and we give them a
highly-complicated piece of music,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.260
they're more likely to make a mistake
if they're sight-reading it,

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.750
than if they've had an
opportunity to rehearse.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.010
- The crux of the problem

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.930
is that surgeons, unfortunately,

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.425
don't have a way to
learn outside of the OR.

00:01:22.425 --> 00:01:24.289
- It is as heavy
as I am.

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:25.122
There we go.

00:01:25.122 --> 00:01:25.955
- Whoa!
- Wow.

00:01:25.955 --> 00:01:30.000
- We use both MRIs and CTs
and they can tell you different things.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.240
So this process is called segmentation.
We end up with a 3D image of Tom's organs.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.200
We send the "Digital Twin" to
the physician that orders it,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.930
so that they can review
and approve that design,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.870
and allow for production
to begin on that piece.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.210
- I've never, ever taken
my shirt off on camera...

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.260
but that's me!

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.690
- That is you.
- I recognize that.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.510
- So this is the upper
part of the abdomen.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.420
This is your liver and
you can see your spleen here.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.380
- That's sticky.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.477
I kind of assumed that
'that' would feel like 'that',

00:02:00.477 --> 00:02:03.840
and it doesn't. They've got
completely different textures.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.480
- I mean it's the closest you're
going to get to a real abdomen.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.840
- So they have artificially given
me a cyst on my left kidney --

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.250
on this thing's left kidney!

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.083
- That's correct.

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:15.030
- You're going to go in
and try and remove it.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.290
- With your permission, I will.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.630
- The camera gets inserted
by the technicians.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.180
So for a patient who is
under robotic surgery,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.675
they would have those
three ports inserted there

00:02:23.675 --> 00:02:24.827
and then stitch it
back after?

00:02:24.827 --> 00:02:26.493
- Yes, sir.
Absolutely. Absolutely.

00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:29.340
So this is a depiction of
the left side of the abdomen,

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.270
and, as you can see here,

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.130
we have your kidney,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.080
and you also have the collecting system,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.540
which is depicted by this
yellow structure here,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.210
and then you have the blue structure
which depicts the vein,

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.790
and the red structure
which depicts the artery.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.150
- We originally made our models
more realistic in color,

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.130
so that they looked
more like real tissue,

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.450
but the feedback we got,

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.780
is part of what the surgeons wanted,
was to have clear demarcations.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.030
This is very clearly
where this structure is,

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.950
and this is very clearly another.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.870
- This is a skeletonized version

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.300
of what you would see in
an actual real setting.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.450
Typically, there's a lot of what we call
intermediate stratum,

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.420
which actually exists
between the actual organs

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.450
in the vessels of the body,
and it's like connective tissues.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.540
It involves fascia as well as fat.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:17.190
- So every movement you make there
is copied at a tiny scale by the robot.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.910
- That's correct. So we can scale this down
to one fifth of the human movement.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.110
So one of the advantages of the robot

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.390
is that it allows the
surgeons to operate longer

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:27.270
into their six and seventh
decades of life, even.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.800
If people have tremors, et cetera.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.466
- You can't see this 'cause you're
looking at the machine,

00:03:30.466 --> 00:03:32.730
but I just, kind of instinctively,
grabbed my own abdomen there.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.178
Just, that's...

00:03:34.178 --> 00:03:35.160
...that's in there.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:35.993
That's me!

00:03:35.993 --> 00:03:36.826
- That is you, you know.

00:03:36.826 --> 00:03:37.950
- They've switched off
a few things, right?

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.290
So I don't have stomach
or intestines there,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.180
and it's not bleeding everywhere.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.770
- We do make models that bleed.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.030
The most common thing we do

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:49.890
is have a hollow artery or vein system
that's filled with blood,

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.260
and when you make a cut,
you'll initially get a bleed

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.270
as it drains out of that system,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.070
but then, because it's
not hooked up to pumps,

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.960
and all of this added complication,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.550
it doesn't continue to bleed.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.280
- What he did do, was he
made it a little interesting,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.010
and he created what we call a renal cyst,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.560
which is a thin-walled, fluid-filled sac.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.030
He made this quite challenging for me

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.870
in that he positioned it
behind your vasculature,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.600
and that's the lovely part
about being able to rehearse this

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.920
prior to the surgery.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:17.910
- This is fascinating!

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.580
- You know, so as a person
is actually being positioned,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:21.720
and put to sleep,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:22.920
I'm actually doing a rehearsal.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:23.753
So what it does,

00:04:23.753 --> 00:04:26.820
is it helps me to fine tune
my visuospatial perception,

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.540
as well as be able to know
how to position my hand on the robot.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.750
All of that stuff helps to
improve efficiency and accuracy

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.030
when you're actually doing the
real dissection of a kidney.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.180
The difference with the robot,
unlike with a human hand,

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.770
you don't have tactile sensation.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.020
So I don't, when I touch this,
I don't know how that really feels,

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.050
but I can look at the tissue
tension when I press here.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:47.220
This is okay.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.253
This is way too much.

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:49.350
- I know it's not a voodoo doll,

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.780
but you did that and something
in my guts went, ooh.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.897
- [laughs]

00:04:52.897 --> 00:04:53.730
- Oh!

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:56.940
- It's nice to have tissue that
pretty much acts like real tissue.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.530
- With one of our collaborators,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:03.540
they actually used a robotic arm
with a scalpel and a force sensor.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.010
So we took animal tissue,
this was a pig kidney,

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:11.250
and this arm would make
a cut in that kidney,

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.740
and it would measure the
forces that are experienced

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.360
by that scalpel tip.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.120
We could then do the exact
same thing to measure

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.000
and then we can iteratively
improve these materials

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.220
to make them behave like real tissue.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.650
- All the 3D printing nerds
are going to be asking me,

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.920
how on earth they 3D printed
soft tissue like this

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.510
and they will not tell me.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.050
- Unfortunately, that's a trade secret
and I cannot comment on that!

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.510
- This is the cyst itself,
and see how this tissue here,

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.060
this is like your peeling
layers of a grape.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.130
- And that is a fluid-filled sac.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:41.963
- Yes.

00:05:41.963 --> 00:05:44.160
- They wouldn't show me how they've
3D printed a fluid-filled thing,

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:44.993
but they have.

00:05:44.993 --> 00:05:48.180
You can see the little striations
where it's been laid down.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.045
You can see the 3D printed parts.
- You sure can.

00:05:50.045 --> 00:05:52.230
So I typically like to
remove it as one whole cyst,

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.440
but oftentimes this is not
infected fluid or cancerous,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.060
you know, this is usually benign.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.380
So it's more style points.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.323
They're separating nicely here
but sometimes it's really adhered.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:03.497
- Wow!

00:06:03.497 --> 00:06:04.374
Oh. Oh, there we go.
- Oh. That's okay.

00:06:04.374 --> 00:06:05.207
There we go.

00:06:05.207 --> 00:06:06.040
That's fine.
- There we go.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:07.770
This is, A, why surgeons rehearse,

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.110
and, B, that, I assume,
would not have been critical.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.460
- No, no, not at all. No.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.190
- For every procedure,
there are different things

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.900
that you need to be worried about.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.330
Sometimes it's that a
structure you're operating on

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.160
is right next to a major vessel.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.410
Sometimes it's that it's next to nerves.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.660
Sometimes it's that
there's a time constraint,

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.820
because you have to clamp an artery

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:30.240
and, at that point, that
organ will stop getting oxygen

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.700
and you are in fact on a clock.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.870
- Our goal is to help
surgeons reduce the risk,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.870
reduce their challenges,
and empower them.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.280
By empowering the surgeons,

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.100
we are also empowering the patients
because they know

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.820
that when their surgeon has
rehearsed ahead of the time,

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:49.707
they are operating with confidence.

00:06:49.707 --> 00:06:51.373
- And that's it,
and it's coming out now.

00:06:51.373 --> 00:06:52.230
- Yep. That's it, right there.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:53.188
That's your cyst.
- Wow.

00:06:53.188 --> 00:06:56.134
Okay. I don't know how I feel about this.

00:06:56.967 --> 00:06:58.530
- Wow!

00:06:58.530 --> 00:06:59.940
That's pretty nice, there.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:00.900
This is perfect, you know.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.370
- Thank you for removing my cyst.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:03.455
- Oh, you're very welcome.

00:07:03.455 --> 00:07:04.921
Thank you. Yeah.

